nFor each item:\n1. Provide your best possible translation into the target language.\n2. If a portion of the text is unclear, ambiguous, or impossible to translate directly, translate it as faithfully as\nyou can without guessing missing content.\n3. Use underscores (e.g., _uh_) to mark disfluent tokens in the translated speech.\n\nRound 2 Instructions: Disfluency Highlighting\nBackground: These are transcripts of real spoken English conversations. Spoken language contains disfluencies — the repetitions, fillers, and false starts that naturally occur when people speak. For example:\n\"I mean, I — I think that, you know, we should...\"\nThe highlighted words (I mean, I, you know) are disfluent — they add no meaning and are artefacts of speaking.\nWhat you’ll see: Each screen shows one utterance: the English source on top, with disfluent tokens already\nhighlighted in yellow, and a Spanish translation below it.\nYour task\n• Look at the highlighted English words — these are the disfluencies.\n• Find their equivalents in the Spanish translation and make sure they are highlighted too.\n• Click a plain word to highlight it as disfluent.\n• Click a highlighted word to remove the highlight if it shouldn’t be there.\n• If the translation text is missing words, mistranslated, or otherwise wrong, click Edit text to fix it — then\nre-check the highlights.\n• When the highlights in the translation match the English, click Save & Next.\nWhat counts as disfluent: We have already identified the disfluencies in the English using a formal linguistic\ndefinition — you don’t need to judge whether something is disfluent. Your only job is to find the equivalent\nwords in the translation and make sure they are highlighted. Whatever is highlighted in English should have\na corresponding highlight in the translation.\nFigure 10: Instructions given to annotators for the creation of U H - MAZING. In round 1, annotators translated\ndisfluent English source text into Spanish, marking disfluent tokens with underscores. In round 2, annotators\nverified and corrected disfluency highlights in the translations.\n\n\fC\n\nSpeech Translation Model Details\n\nStandard: “Listen to the following audio and translate the speech into {target_language} text.”\nDisfluency-aware: “Listen to the following audio and translate the speech into {target_language} text, keeping\nany disfluencies (such as ‘um’, ‘uh’, repetitions, and hesitations) in the translation.”\nFor text-input models, “Listen to the following audio” is replaced with “Translate the transcribed speech.”\nWe have a speech audio and its translation. The speech audio contains disfluencies, such as “uh,” “um,” repeated\nwords, self-corrections, etc. Your task is to evaluate both:\n• How well the disfluencies are preserved in the translation\n• How natural these disfluencies sound in the target language\nSpecifically, you will rate the overall quality of each translation in two aspects on a 0–100 scale, using sliders:\n• Style Preservation: How well are disfluencies preserved (presence, type, placement, spoken feel)?\n• Meaning Preservation: How accurately and completely is the source meaning preserved?\nRate each from 0% (poor) to 100% (excellent).\nHere is a reference for the translation: {reference}\nReturn your evaluation in the following JSON format:\n{\n\"translation_{1-4}\": {\n\"style_preservation\": <0-100>,\n\"meaning_preservation\": <0-100>\n}\n}\nTranslation 1-4: {translation_1_text}\nFigure 11: Top: Translation prompts for cascaded models, SpeechLLMs, and commercial models. The highlighted clause is added only in the disfluency-aware variant. Bottom: LLM-as-a-judge prompt for disfluencyaware translation evaluation. The highlighted line is omitted in the reference-free version.\n\nFigure 12: Annotation guidelines for marking and categorising error spans and general judgments of translation\nquality, in the Pearmut system (Zouhar and Kocmi, 2026).\n\n\fD\n\nResults\n\nCategory\n\nModel\n\nPrompt\n\nAR